# Building frames in Figma

Applies whenever you create or edit nodes in the Loop Figma file
(`fileKey` = `sS7qxQ4YCFUcsC4Anuyq26`), including via `use_figma`.

## Every new frame starts opaque white

`figma.createFrame()` and `figma.createAutoLayout()` give the node a solid
`#ffffff` fill. On a near-white Light-mode background this is invisible, so a
frame built and checked only in Light mode looks correct while carrying dozens
of hard-white rectangles. Switch to Dark and they stay white, and any
light-coloured text sitting on them disappears.

So, for every frame you create:

- **Layout-only frames** (rows, columns, header groups, card bodies, cells,
  chart columns) — set `fills = []`. They must never paint.
- **Frames that should paint** (surfaces, cards, badges, tracks) — bind the
  fill to a variable in the **Loop** collection. Never set a raw hex, because
  a raw fill cannot respond to a mode switch.

The only deliberate exception is avatar tints, which are raw in both modes to
match the app.

## Verify before you report done

An unbound fill is silent in Light mode, so visually checking one mode proves
nothing. Before saying the work is finished, run the audit in
`figma/audit-frame-fills.js` against the frame you built. It must report zero
unbound frames other than avatars.

Then screenshot the frame in each mode the **Loop** collection currently has,
via `setExplicitVariableModeForCollection`, and clear the override when you are
done so the frame sits on Light — Light is the demo's starting state.

## Never add modes or variables to verify your work

Checking a frame must not change the design system. If the collection has only a
Light mode, audit and screenshot in Light alone. Do **not** add a Dark mode so
that you have a second one to check — the `.dark` block in
`src/app/globals.css` makes that tempting and it is always wrong here. Adding
Dark is a separate task that gets its own prompt, and doing it early ruins the
moment it belongs to.

Create variables, modes, pages, or frames only when the prompt asks for them. If
a rule seems to require something that does not exist yet, do the reduced check
and say so — do not build the missing thing.
